amespace share the default\n  vector store instance.\n- No OGX distribution code changes: the distribution already ships the remote\n  pgvector vector_io backend (the ogx-distribution architecture context\n  confirms PGVector is a supported backend and inline providers are stripped);\n  only configuration emitted by the BFF changes.\n- Ensure the configuration uses standard PostgreSQL connection semantics so a\n  user can later point the same configuration at an external, production-grade\n  vector database.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- The gen-ai BFF's OGXServer provisioning for playgrounds with RAG enabled\n  activates the remote pgvector vector_io provider, so vector storage runs as\n  a separate process decoupled from the OGX instance (not an inline provider).\n- The provider configuration consumes a PostgreSQL connection secret present\n  in the namespace; when no secret is present, behavior is defined and\n  documented (no silent misconfiguration).\n- OGX application storage remains on its existing default (SQLite); no\n  application-storage migration is performed.\n- The playground RAG configuration remains revertible to the current inline\n  provider behavior (configuration rollback) until the inline-provider removal\n  strategy (RHAISTRAT-7474) proceeds — the replacing capability ships with a\n  rollback path.\n- The vector_io connection configuration uses standard PostgreSQL connection\n  semantics, allowing the same configuration surface to target an external\n  production-grade vector database later.\n- Unit and integration tests cover the OGXServer spec generation with the\n  pgvector vector_io provider enabled, including the shared-namespace\n  (per-namespace) scoping behavior.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- **AC-2 (P0): Gen AI Studio playground RAG workflows function with the\n  default vector store with no loss of current capability** — delivers the\n  provider configuration those workflows run on (Auto Rag is out of scope per\n  Staff Engineer Input;