{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-980c6f80-854e-49fd-aa63-621c510ed76d","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-9c187efd7868949e","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8004-rzeo1j2a/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-B-E004.md","content":"# BFF-managed PostgreSQL/pgvector provisioning for Gen AI Studio playgrounds (path B)\n\n## Description\n\nConditional branch B of the default-vector-store strategy, executed if the\nRHOAI 3.5 provisioning-path go/no-go decision selects the BFF-managed path —\ni.e. the RHOAI-controlled operator layer from RHAISTRAT-7164 is not available\nin time for 3.5. This epic has the gen-ai BFF provision the\nPostgreSQL-with-pgvector instance directly, similar to how the BFF already\nprovisions guardrails infrastructure: when a playground with RAG is\nconfigured and no suitable instance exists in the namespace, the BFF creates\nthe PostgreSQL Deployment, Service, PVC, and Secret. Because the BFF is not\nan operator with a reconciliation loop, lifecycle management (cleanup on\nplayground deletion, pod-restart recovery, credential rotation) receives\nexplicit design attention, and the implementation is designed for\nportability to the platform-provided shared PostgreSQL planned for a later\nRHOAI release.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Implement BFF-managed provisioning: when a playground with RAG is\n  configured, create the PostgreSQL Deployment, Service, PVC, and Secret in\n  the namespace if no suitable instance already exists (per-namespace\n  scoping; idempotent creation that reuses an existing instance).\n- Use the existing Red Hat PostgreSQL image that ships pgvector\n  (`registry.redhat.io/rhel9/postgresql-16`); reference it through the\n  platform's related-image mechanism so disconnected installs work.\n- Design and implement lifecycle handling appropriate to a non-operator\n  controller: cleanup when the playground is deleted, recovery from\n  PostgreSQL pod restarts, and a documented approach to credential rotation.\n- Design for portability: when the platform-provided shared PostgreSQL\n  becomes available (planned RHOAI 3.6), the BFF consumes the shared\n  connection string instead of self-provisioning — the provisioning decision\n  must be isolatable behind a connection-resolution step.\n- Set initial resource requests/limits from the model-registry-operator's\n  PostgreSQL sizing as the starting point.\n- Wire the provisioned connection secret into the playground's OGXServer\n  pgvector vector_io provider configuration delivered by this strategy.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- When a Gen AI Studio playground with RAG is configured in a namespace with\n  no existing suitable PostgreSQL instance, the BFF creates the Deployment,\n  Service, PVC, and Secret for a pgvector-enabled PostgreSQL instance, with\n  no manual user setup.\n- Provisioning is idempotent: an existing suitable instance in the namespace\n  is reused, not duplicated; playgrounds in the same namespace share one\n  instance (per-namespace isolation).\n- An end-to-end playground RAG workflow (ingest, retrieve, chat) functions\n  against the BFF-provisioned store via the pgvector vector_io provider\n  configuration.\n- Lifecycle behavior is implemented and tested: the provisioned resources\n  are cleaned up when the playground is deleted, the playground recovers\n  from a PostgreSQL pod restart, and the credential-rotation approach is\n  documented and implemented.\n- The connection-resolution step is isolatable: the design demonstrates\n  swapping self-provisioning for a platform-provided shared connection\n  string without rewriting the playground configuration surface.\n- Resource requests/limits on the provisioned instance start from the\n  model-registry-operator's PostgreSQL sizing.\n- The image reference resolves on disconnected installs (via the platform's\n  related-image mechanism for the PostgreSQL 16 image).\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- **AC-1 (P0): default vector store available out-of-the-box without manual\n  provisioning or configuration of external infrastructure** — this branch is\n  the fallback conditional implementation of that requirement when the\n  operator layer is not available.\n- **AC-2 (P0): playground RAG workflows function with the default vector\n  store with no loss of current capability** — end-to-end functional\n  acceptance criteria above (independent validation is carried by the\n  strategy's validation-and-benchmark epic).\n- **AC-4 (P1): scoped appropriately for multi-tenant environments\n  (per-namespace isolation)** — implemented by the per-namespace\n  create-if-absent provisioning model.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":75763,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-ab8e00735ca33645","agent_id":"a0c69221424ccf6a8","session_id":"afd4d759-6499-44cb-acf8-c98d0b579249","uuid":"e47849bf-64d2-4839-acee-16725869ebb6","timestamp":"2026-10-23T19:11:40.292Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8004 into epics"}
