{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8ff84cee72af9e24","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8004\n3\tepic_count: 5\n4\tcritical_path_length: 2\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\t---\n9\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-8004 (Default Vector Store for Gen AI Studio)\n10\t\n11\t## Strategy Structure Notes\n12\t\n13\tThe strategy's full technical detail is stored as a Jira attachment\n14\t(`RHAISTRAT-8004-strategy.md`) that is not present in this workspace, so the\n15\tformal Affected Components / Impacted Teams tables and the numbered HLR list\n16\twere not available. The HLR set below is reconstructed from the strategy's\n17\tAcceptance Criteria and Open Questions, re-prioritized against the Staff\n18\tEngineer Input. This is recorded as a health warning, not a blocker: the Staff\n19\tEngineer Input section is non-empty and takes precedence, and it resolves most\n20\tof the strategy's structure (scope corrections, architectural direction,\n21\tfallback paths, and de-scoping decisions).\n22\t\n23\t### Staff Engineer Input overrides (traceability)\n24\t\n25\tThe Staff Engineer Input diverges from the AI-generated strategy in several\n26\tplaces; the Staff Engineer Input was used throughout:\n27\t\n28\t| # | Strategy (AI-generated) position | Staff Engineer Input override | Effect on decomposition |\n29\t|---|----------------------------------|-------------------------------|-------------------------|\n30\t| 1 | \"Every LlamaStackDistribution instance requires a PostgreSQL database\" (extension of existing infra) | OGX defaults to SQLite; the odh-dashboard gen-ai BFF hardcodes SQLite (`llamastack_config.go`). Introducing PostgreSQL is **new work** | PostgreSQL provisioning modeled as new implementation work, not a config tweak |\n31\t| 2 | Custom pgvector image build + Konflux onboarding, 1-2 sprints | `registry.redhat.io/rhel9/postgresql-16` already includes pgvector; model-registry-operator already auto-provisions it; `RELATED_IMAGE_POSTGRESQL_16_IMAGE` already in the rhods-operator CSV | No image-build epic, no `konflux-onboarding` epic, no `repo-onboarding`/`license-validation` chain — eliminated |\n32\t| 3 | Auto Rag in scope (affected components, ACs, validation) | Decouple from Auto Rag — validate pgvector only for Gen AI Studio Playground RAG; Auto Rag compatibility moves to RHAISTRAT-7824 (RHOAI 3.6) | Auto Rag removed from all epics, ACs, and validation scope |\n33\t| 4 | Latency thresholds / corpus size / index-type open questions | Dev-experimentation scope: no strict SLAs; benchmark verifies same order of magnitude vs inline Milvus baseline; flat scan sufficient | Those open questions removed; single benchmark/validation epic, no Investigation |\n34\t| 5 | Consolidate vector + application storage; SQLite→PostgreSQL upgrade path | Vector storage (`remote::pgvector` via `ENABLE_PGVECTOR`) and application storage are independent; application storage stays SQLite | No storage-migration epic or AC; scope limited to the vector_io provider |\n35\t| 6 | (implicit) single provisioning approach | Two viable paths gated on RHAISTRAT-7164 (operator layer) landing for 3.5: operator-managed vs BFF-managed provisioning, with bring-your-own PostgreSQL as the final fallback; go/no-go gate to be defined in sprint planning | Investigation epic + conditional decomposition branches |\n36\t\n37\t## Epic List\n38\t\n39\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n40\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n41\t| RHAISTRAT-8004-E001 | Investigation: RHOAI 3.5 provisioning-path go/no-go for the default vector store | Investigation | dashboard-crimson-scrum | P0 |\n42\t| RHAISTRAT-8004-E002 | Enable the remote pgvector vector_io provider for Gen AI Studio playgrounds | Implementation | dashboard-crimson-scrum | P0 |\n43\t| RHAISTRAT-8004-E003 | Validate and benchmark Gen AI Studio playground RAG on the default pgvector store | Implementation | dashboard-crimson-scrum | P0 |\n44\t| RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-A-E004 | Operator-managed PostgreSQL/pgvector provisioning for Gen AI Studio (path A) | Implementation | dashboard-crimson-scrum | P0 |\n45\t| RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-B-E004 | BFF-managed PostgreSQL/pgvector provisioning for Gen AI Studio playgrounds (path B) | Implementation | dashboard-crimson-scrum | P0 |\n46\t\n47\tMain plan: E001–E003 (3 epics). Branch epics: BRANCH-A-E004 and BRANCH-B-E004\n48\tare conditional alternatives attached to the E001 gate; exactly one is executed.\n49\t`epic_count=5` counts all epics including branches.\n50\t\n51\t## Dependency DAG\n52\t\n53\t```mermaid\n54\tgraph TD\n55\t    E001[\"E001 Investigation: provisioning-path go/no-go\"] --> E003[\"E003 Validation + benchmark\"]\n56\t    E002[\"E002 pgvector vector_io provider config\"] --> E003\n57\t    E001 --> BRANCHA[\"BRANCH-A-E004 Operator-managed provisioning (path A)\"]\n58\t    E001 --> BRANCHB[\"BRANCH-B-E004 BFF-managed provisioning (path B)\"]\n59\t    E002 --> BRANCHB\n60\t```\n61\t\n62\tBranch epics are excluded from the main-plan critical path. Main-plan critical\n63\tpath length: 2 (E001 → E003, or E002 → E003).\n64\t\n65\t## DAG Justification\n66\t\n67\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n68\t|------|------|-----------|\n69\t| E001 → E003 | Rule 3 | The go/no-go outcome determines which provisioning path the validation campaign exercises; validation scope changes with the branch (`rewrite`) |\n70\t| E002 → E003 | Rule 12/13 | The validation and benchmark build against the pgvector vector_io configuration E002 delivers; the benchmark measures the surface E002 configures |\n71\t| E001 → BRANCH-A-E004 | Rule 3 | True gate: path A exists only if the RHOAI-controlled operator layer (RHAISTRAT-7164) lands for 3.5 (`remove` if not) |\n72\t| E001 → BRANCH-B-E004 | Rule 3 | True gate: path B is the selected alternative when the operator layer is not available (`remove` if path A is chosen) |\n73\t| E002 → BRANCH-B-E004 | Rule 12 | Both changes land in the gen-ai BFF's playground provisioning flow: the BFF-provisioned database secret is wired into the vector_io provider configuration E002 introduces — serializing avoids two epics building the same provisioning surface |\n74\t| (no edge) E002 ∥ E001 | Rule 14/15 | Independent work: the vector_io provider configuration is identical regardless of which party provisions the database; the interface (a PostgreSQL connection secret) is stable across branches |\n75\t| (no edge) E002 ∥ BRANCH-A-E004 | Rule 13 (config reference) | The operator-provisioned database publishes a connection secret that the BFF's vector_io configuration consumes — a configuration reference, not a build-against artifact; coupling is expressed as an AC on BRANCH-A-E004 (\"connection secret consumable by the playground's OGXServer vector_io configuration\") |\n76\t| (no edge) BRANCH-A-E004 ∥ BRANCH-B-E004 | conditional branches | Mutually exclusive alternatives selected by the E001 gate; never both executed |\n77\t\n78\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n79\t\n80\tHLRs are reconstructed from the strategy's Acceptance Criteria (AC-1…AC-6)\n81\tand Open Questions (OQ-2) because the formal HLR table is in the unavailable\n82\tstrategy attachment. Priorities derive from the strategy's Blocker priority\n83\tand Staff Engineer Input emphasis.\n84\t\n85\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n86\t|-----|----------|---------|-------|\n87\t| AC-1: Default vector store available out-of-the-box, no manual provisioning/configuration of external infrastructure | P0 | BRANCH-A-E004, BRANCH-B-E004 (gated by E001) | The provisioning capability is exactly one conditional branch depending on the go/no-go outcome; both branches deliver the same HLR |\n88\t| AC-2: Gen AI Studio playground RAG workflows function with the default vector store, no loss of current capability | P0 | E002, BRANCH-A-E004, BRANCH-B-E004, E003 | Provider configuration (E002), end-to-end functional ACs on the provisioning branches, independent validation (E003). Auto Rag was removed from this HLR per Staff Engineer Input (covered by RHAISTRAT-7824) |\n89\t| AC-3: Default vector store runs as a separate process, decoupled from the Llama Stack instance (not inline) | P0 | E002 (with provisioning branches) | Satisfied by configuring the remote `pgvector` vector_io provider instead of the inline Milvus provider; the ogx-distribution already ships the remote pgvector backend (no OGX code change) |\n90\t| AC-4: Scoped appropriately for multi-tenant environments (per-namespace isolation) | P1 | BRANCH-A-E004, BRANCH-B-E004 | Per-namespace scoping is the direction given in the Staff Engineer Input (Silas Brenmark's preference; the Auto Rag per-instance concern is moot after de-scoping Auto Rag); both branches implement one instance per namespace |\n91\t| AC-5: Lightweight enough for resource-constrained environments without significant overhead | P2 | E003 | **bundled**: resource-sizing validation is part of the same benchmark/validation campaign as AC-2 — same instrumented playground RAG runs, same report; splitting would duplicate the campaign. Sizing starts from the model-registry-operator's PostgreSQL requests/limits per Staff Engineer Input |\n92\t| OQ-2: Migration path from the default vector store to a production-grade vector database | P2 | E002 | **bundled**: migration readiness is a property of the same connection-configuration surface E002 builds (standard PostgreSQL connection semantics); for 3.5 there is no separately schedulable migration deliverable — the Staff Engineer Input repositions the default store as dev-experimentation scope with external vector DBs for production workloads. Explicit migration tooling is deferred (health warning) |\n93\t\n94\tFull coverage: every HLR maps to at least one epic; no orphaned requirements.\n95\tPriority-split analysis: AC-4 (P1) and AC-5/OQ-2 (P2) were evaluated for\n96\tsplitting into separate epics — all three are bundled (see flags above) because\n97\teach shares the implementation or validation surface of its P0 carrier;\n98\tnone could ship as an independently valuable epic without double-touching the\n99\tsame code or campaign.\n100\t\n101\t## Health Warnings\n102\t\n103\t1. **Strategy attachment unavailable.** The full strategy\n104\t   (`RHAISTRAT-8004-strategy.md` attachment) is not present in this workspace;\n105\t   the HLR set, component/team tables, and formal HLR priorities were\n106\t   reconstructed from the summary sections and Staff Engineer Input. The\n107\t   Affected Components and Impacted Teams tables could not be read — team\n108\t   assignment (`dashboard-crimson-scrum`) is inferred from the strategy's\n109\t   labels.\n110\t2. **Externally-controlled gate timing.** RHAISTRAT-7164 (dashboard operator\n111\t   modularization, In Progress) is a hard dependency for the preferred\n112\t   provisioning path, and its landing date is outside this strategy's control.\n113\t   Per Staff Engineer Input, the strategy cannot commit to a specific release\n114\t   until the go/no-go gate resolves — this is a schedule risk on E001, not an\n115\t   acceptance criterion.\n116\t3. **Auto Rag decoupling.** The strategy's summary, ACs, and validation\n117\t   requirements mention Auto Rag; the Staff Engineer Input removes it from\n118\t   scope (RHAISTRAT-7824, RHOAI 3.6). Downstream consumers of this\n119\t   decomposition must not re-add Auto Rag validation to these epics.\n120\t4. **Migration path deferred.** OQ-2 (Bram Sundqvist's migration-path\n121\t   concern) is addressed only directionally (standard connection semantics on\n122\t   E002). If product later requires an explicit migration story (export/import\n123\t   from pgvector to production vector DBs), it needs its own strategy.\n124\t5. **BFF lifecycle risk (path B).** The gen-ai BFF is not an operator; cleanup\n125\t   on playground deletion, pod-restart recovery, and credential rotation need\n126\t   explicit design attention (Staff Engineer Input). If this design exceeds a\n127\t   sprint, apply the re-split trigger on BRANCH-B-E004 rather than letting it\n128\t   balloon: **re-split trigger** — split lifecycle/credential-rotation\n129\t   hardening into a follow-up epic if the BFF-managed design exceeds one\n130\t   sprint; create-on-first-use provisioning must land first.\n131\t6. **Bring-your-own fallback is a degraded experience.** If neither\n132\t   operator-managed nor BFF-managed provisioning proves viable for 3.5, the\n133\t   final fallback (users manually configure the OGXServer CR spec with their\n134\t   own PostgreSQL) breaks the out-of-the-box AC-1. That fallback is recorded\n135\t   in the branch gate metadata, not planned as an epic.\n136\t\n137\t## Tiered Delivery / Conditional Decomposition\n138\t\n139\tThe go/no-go unknown (will the RHOAI-controlled operator layer from\n140\tRHAISTRAT-7164 be available for RHOAI 3.5?) has two bounded outcomes, each\n141\tchanging which downstream epic exists. It is modeled as conditional\n142\tdecomposition branches off the E001 Investigation:\n143\t\n144\t- **Branch A** (`RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-A-E004`): the operator layer lands in\n145\t  time → PostgreSQL/pgvector lifecycle management implemented in that operator\n146\t  layer following the model-registry-operator pattern.\n147\t  `gate_failure_impact: remove` — if the operator layer is not available for\n148\t  3.5, this path does not exist.\n149\t- **Branch B** (`RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-B-E004`): the operator layer does not\n150\t  land in time → the gen-ai BFF provisions the PostgreSQL/pgvector instance\n151\t  directly (Deployment, Service, PVC, Secret), mirroring how the BFF already\n152\t  provisions guardrails infrastructure, designed for portability to the\n153\t  platform-provided shared PostgreSQL planned for RHOAI 3.6.\n154\t  `gate_failure_impact: remove` — if path A is selected, this path is not\n155\t  built; if BFF-managed provisioning proves unviable in design, the recorded\n156\t  fallback is requiring bring-your-own PostgreSQL configured on the OGXServer\n157\t  CR spec.\n158\t\n159\tBranch E002 (vector_io provider configuration) and E003 (validation and\n160\tbenchmark) are main-plan epics because they are needed under every outcome;\n161\tE003 is `gated_by` E001 (it validates whichever branch is selected) and must\n162\texecute after the selected branch's provisioning lands — expressed as an\n163\tacceptance criterion on E003, since branch epics are not main-plan DAG nodes.\n164\t\n165\t## Component Mapping Notes\n166\t\n167\tAll epics use the canonical component **Gen AI Studio**. The work spans the\n168\todh-dashboard repository (gen-ai BFF in `packages/gen-ai`, and, under branch\n169\tA, the operator layer delivered by RHAISTRAT-7164); Gen AI Studio is the\n170\tsingle most-relevant canonical name for the whole vertical slice. OGX\n171\tcomponents are **passive** in this strategy — the ogx-distribution already\n172\tships the remote pgvector vector_io backend and strips inline providers\n173\t(corroborated by `.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/ogx-distribution.md`),\n174\tso no OGX code change is needed; the existing remote vector store\n175\timplementation is reused per the Staff Engineer Input. Validation of that\n176\tpassive component is an acceptance criterion on E002/E003 (same team), so no\n177\tseparate validation epic is generated. No new components are introduced\n178\t(the PostgreSQL image is an existing Red Hat image, per Staff Engineer Input\n179\tCorrection 2), so no onboarding chain is required.\n180\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9c0dd92e9b510fbb","agent_id":"a092b6ce1ea68b3fa","session_id":"afd4d759-6499-44cb-acf8-c98d0b579249","uuid":"e8243fca-0010-4062-8c1d-e81752d989de","timestamp":"2026-10-23T19:17:49.199Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-8ff84cee72af9e24","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type"