{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9f4558668c473997","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-B-E004\n3\ttitle: BFF-managed PostgreSQL/pgvector provisioning for Gen AI Studio playgrounds\n4\t  (path B)\n5\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8004\n6\tcomponent: Gen AI Studio\n7\tteam: dashboard-crimson-scrum\n8\ttype: Implementation\n9\tpriority: P0\n10\tbranch: B\n11\tgated_by: RHAISTRAT-8004-E001\n12\tdependencies:\n13\t- RHAISTRAT-8004-E001\n14\t- RHAISTRAT-8004-E002\n15\tgate_failure_impact:\n16\t  action: remove\n17\t  fallback_approach: If the operator layer lands in time for RHOAI 3.5, path A is\n18\t    selected and this path is removed; if BFF-managed provisioning proves unviable\n19\t    in design (lifecycle complexity of a non-operator controller), fall back to requiring\n20\t    users to bring their own PostgreSQL configured directly on the OGXServer CR spec.\n21\tai_signals:\n22\t  change_specificity: 1\n23\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n24\t  adapter_pattern: 1\n25\t  existing_foundation: 1\n26\t  open_questions: -1\n27\t  external_dependency: 0\n28\t  human_process_gates: 0\n29\t  repo_access: 1\n30\t  architecture_claims: 1\n31\timplementation_type: null\n32\tinvestigation_signals: null\n33\tai_implementability: High\n34\tai_implementability_score: 5\n35\tjira_key: null\n36\t---\n37\t# BFF-managed PostgreSQL/pgvector provisioning for Gen AI Studio playgrounds (path B)\n38\t\n39\t## Description\n40\t\n41\tConditional branch B of the default-vector-store strategy, executed if the\n42\tRHOAI 3.5 provisioning-path go/no-go decision selects the BFF-managed path —\n43\ti.e. the RHOAI-controlled operator layer from RHAISTRAT-7164 is not available\n44\tin time for 3.5. This epic has the gen-ai BFF provision the\n45\tPostgreSQL-with-pgvector instance directly, similar to how the BFF already\n46\tprovisions guardrails infrastructure: when a playground with RAG is\n47\tconfigured and no suitable instance exists in the namespace, the BFF creates\n48\tthe PostgreSQL Deployment, Service, PVC, and Secret. Because the BFF is not\n49\tan operator with a reconciliation loop, lifecycle management (cleanup on\n50\tplayground deletion, pod-restart recovery, credential rotation) receives\n51\texplicit design attention, and the implementation is designed for\n52\tportability to the platform-provided shared PostgreSQL planned for a later\n53\tRHOAI release.\n54\t\n55\t## Scope\n56\t\n57\t- Implement BFF-managed provisioning: when a playground with RAG is\n58\t  configured, create the PostgreSQL Deployment, Service, PVC, and Secret in\n59\t  the namespace if no suitable instance already exists (per-namespace\n60\t  scoping; idempotent creation that reuses an existing instance).\n61\t- Use the existing Red Hat PostgreSQL image that ships pgvector\n62\t  (`registry.redhat.io/rhel9/postgresql-16`); reference it through the\n63\t  platform's related-image mechanism so disconnected installs work.\n64\t- Design and implement lifecycle handling appropriate to a non-operator\n65\t  controller: cleanup when the playground is deleted, recovery from\n66\t  PostgreSQL pod restarts, and a documented approach to credential rotation.\n67\t- Design for portability: when the platform-provided shared PostgreSQL\n68\t  becomes available (planned RHOAI 3.6), the BFF consumes the shared\n69\t  connection string instead of self-provisioning — the provisioning decision\n70\t  must be isolatable behind a connection-resolution step.\n71\t- Set initial resource requests/limits from the model-registry-operator's\n72\t  PostgreSQL sizing as the starting point.\n73\t- Wire the provisioned connection secret into the playground's OGXServer\n74\t  pgvector vector_io provider configuration delivered by this strategy.\n75\t\n76\t## Acceptance Criteria\n77\t\n78\t- When a Gen AI Studio playground with RAG is configured in a namespace with\n79\t  no existing suitable PostgreSQL instance, the BFF creates the Deployment,\n80\t  Service, PVC, and Secret for a pgvector-enabled PostgreSQL instance, with\n81\t  no manual user setup.\n82\t- Provisioning is idempotent: an existing suitable instance in the namespace\n83\t  is reused, not duplicated; playgrounds in the same namespace share one\n84\t  instance (per-namespace isolation).\n85\t- An end-to-end playground RAG workflow (ingest, retrieve, chat) functions\n86\t  against the BFF-provisioned store via the pgvector vector_io provider\n87\t  configuration.\n88\t- Lifecycle behavior is implemented and tested: the provisioned resources\n89\t  are cleaned up when the playground is deleted, the playground recovers\n90\t  from a PostgreSQL pod restart, and the credential-rotation approach is\n91\t  documented and implemented.\n92\t- The connection-resolution step is isolatable: the design demonstrates\n93\t  swapping self-provisioning for a platform-provided shared connection\n94\t  string without rewriting the playground configuration surface.\n95\t- Resource requests/limits on the provisioned instance start from the\n96\t  model-registry-operator's PostgreSQL sizing.\n97\t- The image reference resolves on disconnected installs (via the platform's\n98\t  related-image mechanism for the PostgreSQL 16 image).\n99\t\n100\t## HLR Traceability\n101\t\n102\t- **AC-1 (P0): default vector store available out-of-the-box without manual\n103\t  provisioning or configuration of external infrastructure** — this branch is\n104\t  the fallback conditional implementation of that requirement when the\n105\t  operator layer is not available.\n106\t- **AC-2 (P0): playground RAG workflows function with the default vector\n107\t  store with no loss of current capability** — end-to-end functional\n108\t  acceptance criteria above (independent validation is carried by the\n109\t  strategy's validation-and-benchmark epic).\n110\t- **AC-4 (P1): scoped appropriately for multi-tenant environments\n111\t  (per-namespace isolation)** — implemented by the per-namespace\n112\t  create-if-absent provisioning model.\n113\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b51940858cd4c94f","agent_id":"a64b04a6e8148f8d2","session_id":"afd4d759-6499-44cb-acf8-c98d0b579249","uuid":"1172fcb6-e1c0-45ea-a7b9-1c3f9b270001","timestamp":"2026-10-23T19:20:08.863Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-9f4558668c473997","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-8004 decomposition"}
